sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch17-04> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "The forms of related organisms can, as a general rule, be mapped onto one another by a simple and symmetrical deformation of a Cartesian coordinate grid, such as would result from a slight and simple change in the system of forces acting on the growing organism.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Related organisms differ by simple, symmetrical Cartesian transformations";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "F_2 = \\phi(F_1), \\phi : (x, y) \\mapsto (x', y') a simple, symmetrical deformation of Cartesian coordinates";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XVII, “On the Theory of Transformations, or the Comparison of Related Forms”; topic “Theory of transformations”. Scope: related species within a zoological class (fishes, crustaceans, reptile and mammal skulls, etc.)"
}
